-- The constant functor: `Const a b` carries an `a` and erases the `b`.
-- It is the universal visitor for computations whose result type is
-- fixed, and the composing piece the Category.* classes use as their
-- lawful constant functor.
--
-- Module name: the module scheme (design/base-library.md) names this
-- module `Data.Const`. The header is the single-segment `Const` pending
-- the dotted-module-name fix: the checker keys imports by an import
-- path's last segment (check_decls.rs `imports_of`), so a
-- `module Data.Const` header cannot be imported from anywhere. The file
-- path is the scheme's; rename the header when the fix lands.
--
-- `do` notation and these classes (decision D22): `do` picks its
-- provider lexically per definition, through a `using` binder with a
-- DirectSequencing/ResultSequencing/OptionSequencing head (default:
-- DirectSequencing). A do-provider is a fixed instance choice: exactly
-- one container per definition, no polymorphism. The Category.*
-- classes (Functor/Applicative/Monad) abstract over containers for
-- polymorphic code; their `fmap`/`pure`/`>>=` are ordinary class
-- methods resolved per use site. The two mechanisms do not interact
-- and neither is changing: `do` never consults the classes, and a class
-- constraint never selects a do provider.
--
-- Why these are plain functions rather than class instances: the
-- Category.* classes cannot be declared in the current surface. The
-- checker rejects type-constructor variables (`f a` for a class
-- parameter `f` fails with "cannot apply non-type-constructor f"), so
-- `class Functor f` with `fmap :: (a -> b) -> f a -> f b` and its
-- instances are unexpressible; the fix is scheduled as a compiler
-- slice (higher-kinded parameters are a requirement of the modules and
-- compilation specification, section 1). `map-const` is the Functor
-- `fmap` body for `Const`, held at the type until the class lands.
-- `Const`'s Applicative bodies are not here: `pure`/`<*>` for `Const`
-- need to combine payloads under the `Algebra.Monoid` constraint, which
-- is another slice's class, so they belong to the `Category.Applicative`
-- instance once both classes exist. The operator `<$>` is a class
-- method per decision D2 (operators are class methods with per-module
-- fixity declarations), so no per-type operator stand-in is defined
-- here.
module Const where

  data Const a b where
    Const :: a -> Const a b

  -- Elimination: the carried value.
  get-const :: forall a b. Const a b -> a
  get-const c = case c of
    Const x -> x

  -- Functor body: the carried value is unchanged and the function is
  -- ignored (the constant functor).
  map-const :: forall m a b. (a -> b) -> Const m a -> Const m b
  map-const f c = case c of
    Const x -> Const x
